dict(id='4.2',sub='Society & Social Justice',nb=['ca','pol'],ch=['pol-ch12'],tag='PM',
 t='Maternity rights: Rakhi Bisht v. Union of India (2026)',
 ctx='Delhi High Court held that availing maternity leave cannot diminish a woman\'s professional status or career prospects; cited Articles 14, 15, 21, 42 and the Code on Social Security, 2020.',
 pts=['Principles: return to same or substantially equivalent role (pay, status, responsibilities, authority, promotion); protection covers duties, reporting, appraisal; explain reasons and alternatives before rejoining if original role is unavailable; no retaliation; Court noted a gap in post-maternity reintegration and told the Centre to frame safeguards within six months.',
 'Code on Social Security 2020 subsumed the Maternity Benefit Act 1961. Applies to factories, mines, plantations and shops/establishments with 10+ employees; eligibility 80 days of work in preceding 12 months.',
 'Leave: 26 weeks for fewer than two surviving children (up to 8 weeks before delivery); 12 weeks for two or more (up to 6 weeks before); adoptive and commissioning mothers 12 weeks; crèche for 50+ employees with four visits daily.',
 'Challenges: Time Use Survey 2024 - 41% of women (15-59) did unpaid caregiving vs 21.4% of men; women 140 min/day vs men 74; employer-cost bias; weak reintegration; informal-sector exclusion (PLFS 2025: 63.1% of female non-agricultural workers in proprietary/partnership enterprises); weak crèches and enforcement.',
 'Way forward: shared parental leave (ILO C156); socialise costs (ILO C183); phased return, flexible work, lactation support; PMMVY; strengthen enforcement.',
 'Connecting news: Tamil Nadu announced 365 days of maternity leave for state employees for a third child (up from 12 weeks). Central Civil Services (Leave) Rules 1972 give male employees 15 days paternity leave; no statutory private-sector paternity leave. Hamsaanandini Nanduri (2026) urged recognising paternity leave as social security.'],
 pro=['CEDAW 1979 Article 11: non-discrimination in employment. ILO C183 (2000) Maternity Protection; ILO C156 (1981) Workers with Family Responsibilities; India has not ratified either.','Cases: MCD v. Female Workers (Muster Roll) 2000; Deepika Singh v. CAT 2022; Hamsaanandini Nanduri v. Union of India 2026 (removed three-month adoption limit).'],
 mains=['Keywords: motherhood penalty, substantive equality, career continuity, reproductive autonomy, care redistribution, workplace reintegration.'],
 kw='Motherhood penalty; Substantive equality; Workplace reintegration',
 pyq='2019 Prelims: Maternity Benefit (Amendment) Act 2017 provisions (crèche visits, leave for two children).'),
